      {
        "id": 0,
        "original_id": "ceee676c-e706-54e2-998b-e6f05dc5a623",
        "text": "This chunk is about:\n- People: King Conchobar, Cúchulainn, Queen Medb, Mary Colum\n- Places: Ulster, Connacht, Ireland, Lowland Scotland\n- Works: Louis le Brocquy Táin illustrations\n- Languages: Scots, Ulster Scots\n- Topics: Táin, Irish literature\n\nFacts:\n- The Táin tells of the exploits of King Conchobar and his chief warrior Cúchulainn, who is known as “The Hound of Ulster.”\n- The Táin tells of Queen Medb of Connacht invading Ulster in an attempt to capture the Brown Bull of Cuailgne.\n- Ulster is a province in the north of the island of Ireland.\n- Scots is a Germanic language variety spoken in Lowland Scotland and in parts of Ulster, where the local dialect is called Ulster Scots.\n- Mary Colum, née Maguire, was born on June 14, 1884, and died on October 22, 1957.\n- Mary Colum was an Irish literary critic and author who also co-founded a literary journal.",
        "type": "TextSummary"
      },
      {
        "id": 1,
